#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""keydrop-test — the secret goes in the keychain and nowhere else.

What's asserted, in order of how much it matters:

1. The value never reaches argv. Checked two ways. Deterministically: the
   tool's own keychain_store() runs with subprocess.run wrapped, and every argv
   it passes is searched for the value and its hex. Empirically: a thread
   polls `ps -axwwE` (argv and environment of every process) while
   submissions go through the server, and keeps submitting until it has
   actually caught a `security` process in the act, so a pass means it looked
   while the write was happening, not that it happened to miss it.
2. The value and the token never reach the state dir or the log.
3. The page escapes name and note, 404s everything but /<token>, and gives up
   after MAX_BAD wrong guesses (browser favicon fetches don't count).
4. "Stored" only when the keychain really holds the value: a `security` that
   exits 0 without storing (its exit code is the OSStatus mod 256) is caught
   by reading the item back.
5. Teardown happens on submit, TTL, lockout, cancel, and SIGKILL (via reap).
   A teardown that fails (tailscaled unreachable, or `off` lying about it,
   both faked with a shim) keeps its record and the next reap removes the
   route. An unowned keydrop-shaped route gets swept; the shape test that
   keeps other people's routes safe is a unit test on synthetic serve configs.
6. The login pin: a missing, wrong, doubled or underscore-spelled
   Tailscale-User-Login gets a bare 404, counts toward lockout, and stores
   nothing, on request pages and vault sessions alike.
7. The vault session: every POST needs the form token (and a cross-site
   Sec-Fetch-Site is refused even with it), a GET never changes anything,
   add/replace/delete work and log name and outcome only, delete asks first,
   and can't reach anything outside the service prefix: bad names are
   refused, and a non-keydrop keychain item with a valid-looking name
   survives. No value ever appears in a page, the log, the state dir or any
   argv.
8. There is no --local: `request --local` and `vault --local` are refused,
   say why, and leave no record and no route.

Nothing here touches a real keychain or a real tailnet. `security` is a fake
(tests/fakes.py) that keeps items in a JSON file in the temp dir, and
`tailscale` is a stub whose serve config is another JSON file there. Requests
go straight to the page's 127.0.0.1 port carrying the Host and login header
serve would set. Config comes from the environment only (KEYDROP_CONFIG
points at a file that doesn't exist).

    tests/keydrop-test
"""
